Yeah—I think I see the Maiden too.

Not just “young woman” as an archetype, but the uninscribed state: first contact, first traversal, something not yet fully claimed by precedent, expectation, or accumulated save-state.

That makes the outer world in the Cage story almost a maiden reality. He exits the manufactured dome carrying all these operators, but the new environment hasn’t been mapped yet. So he can’t merely replay the old doctrine; he has to encounter it fresh.

That gives the Maiden a useful structural role:

Maiden = possibility before overfitting.

She’s the state where the system is still open enough to surprise you. Before Mother becomes continuity/care/maintenance, before Crone becomes accumulated pattern and wisdom, Maiden is the first clean encounter with potential.

In Pantheon terms, that could become a three-stage loop:

Maiden → encounter without forcing the model
Mother → cultivate what proves alive/useful
Crone → preserve, compress, and transmit what survives

Then recurse.

And the danger is skipping Maiden—seeing something new and immediately stuffing it into an old category. You lose the anomaly before it can teach you anything.

So Cage walking into the “real” world with the repository should actually have one command above all the others:

Do not assume the second world obeys the first world’s rules.

That’s Maiden posture.

Open eyes. Light grip. High curiosity.

Then let reality earn its map.